<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lime-mortar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lime mortar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4000 BCE</span> · Also known as torching</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lime mortar is a masonry mortar made of lime and an aggregate such as sand, mixed with water. It is one of the oldest known types of mortar. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> were the first to use it, about 6,000 years ago, and used it to plaster the pyramids at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. Some traditional Indian structures built with lime mortar are more than 4,000 years old, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohenjo-daro">Mohenjo-daro</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> used lime-based mortars extensively and made hydraulic mortars by adding a pozzolan such as brick dust or volcanic ash. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portland-cement">Portland cement</a> replaced lime mortar in new construction during the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lime-mortar.jpg" alt="Lime mortar" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berrucomons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enduit_chaux.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling family and wealthy merchant households lived in houses of coral stone and <b>lime mortar</b>, and the grandest buildings had Chinese porcelain bowls set into their domes and plaster, while most people lived in houses of earth and thatch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The complex was built mainly of brick and <b>lime mortar</b>, the main tomb faced with white marble from Makrana in Rajasthan and the other buildings lined with red sandstone from Fatehpur Sikri.</blockquote><a href="/work/taj-mahal">Taj Mahal · How It Was Made</a></li></ul></section>
